o  depending on the kind of shot that it is  we either have to recreate those bits  that have been included in some way or  or we leveraged visual effects material  in a case of a movie like this there's a  lot of visual effects will get  breakdowns of shots so we'll actually  get a separate background from the  foreground and essentially use those  layers to create a stepped layered  effect and then separately from from the  occluded areas you also have internal  volume right so when you see a person's  face in real life you have a sense of  the curvature of their face you have the  cheeks versus their eyes versus their  ears you know where that stuff is  they're not just a pop-up book they have  actual curvature and you can perceive  that so we go through a process of  adding that volume into the characters  as well so you mentioned you started  working but in 3d if you know years a  couple of years ago or some years ago  when it started coming back into the  fold  has it changed a lot since then it has I  mean it really is interesting as a as a  look 3ds not a new art form I mean some  of the earliest cinema was using was  experimenting with 3d and and certainly  in the 50s it had a big resurgence so  with this sort of gimmicky black and  white 3d that was happening at the time  people kind of refer to that as 3d and  then in the 80s there was another sort  of period of 3d where people were  suddenly leveraging color and polarisers  to be able to separate see didn't have  the red and blue glasses anymore and all  that so every time there's this sort of  technological advance when we go back to  3d and this time around the big change  was really that now we could make it we  could leverage all the modern computing  tools that we have available in making  films to really make an digital  projection and everything else to make  it really clean and a much more pleasant  viewing experience for the audience so  this is not this is not your  grandmother's 3d you know it's really a  completely modern take on what 3d is and  if you think back to I know for most  people  avatar was kind of marked the marking  point although there were some films  before that avatar was the film that  people really look to and they go oh my  god they real wake in 3d for people and  then you started to see 3d films get  released rapidly after that so what  happened if you go back and look at  avatar and compare it to the films that  are being released today  it actually was very conservative and  it's 3d approach at the time it's it  seemed incredibly immersive and amazing  and it's a beautiful film but if you but  even Jim Cameron has said that he would  take it further today and will on the  sequels because the move the the the  whole art form is evolving rapidly this  before as storytellers figure out how to  use it and what to use it for and what  to do with it they're sort of changing  the way they approach it it's  fascinating to watch  so the first film that I did well you  know Alice in Wonderland I worked on was  a you know an awesome 3d experience but  if you look at that movie compared to  the movies were doing today they're much  cleaner much much deeper also so we're  much more able to take it much further  and sort of increase the 3d effect and  it's it's a it just continues to evolve  is the filmmakers figure out how to use  it yeah I mean certainly as an audience  member having seen the film and it feels  so much more seamless you know you can  it's subtle in a way that just like  transports you into the film for Doctor  Strange though is there you know what  what new techniques or innovations or  has there been you know anything in  particular about Doctor Strange that was  you know exciting for you  well the Doctor Strange had some  interesting challenges that that other  Marvel films and and the films I did  before being at Marvel that I hadn't  encountered one of which was that the  visual effect the underlying visual  effects were breaking a lot of they  relied on 2d visual cues to work so if  you think about an MC Escher drawing  he's playing with perspective in a way  that makes you think you seeing one  thing when you're actually seeing  something else and that's the magic  trick of an MC Escher drawing right  we're doing those but in 3d  in the middle of an action sequence and  we have to do it in a way where 3d  doesn't give away the trick how do we do  it how do we tell that story in 3d  without immediately making people aware  that there's something sneaky going on  in the imagery so so we tested that  quite a bit early on and we've been  working on this movie much longer than  we typically do so a lot of times we get  involved with a film fairly late in the  post-production process because we  waiting for the movie to kind of get  completed before we take it on and start  to the conversion process on this film I  was on I went to London and it was on  visiting the set and and we were looking  at previous before they even shot the  movie and consulting with them early  early early on to try and plan out how  this was going to work because 3d was  such a big part of the storytelling of  the film and yet the movie has to work  as a 2d movie too so there's this  interesting kind of balance there of  like not trying to drive the movie into  solving just the 3d solutions but but  how can it work in both worlds and and I  think we I think we found that place on  this movie you it's but you know so that  the visual tricks are very interesting  there's also there's a lot of there's a  lot of big epic moments that needed to  be told in a way that where we could  build and and so you know one of the  things we try to do with 3d is not just  keep it deep all the time keep it at 11  all the time because you sort of  audience fatigues after a while and I  compare it to like a musical score that  if you think about if you just played it  loud constantly it just becomes all the  same whereas if you modulate then people  really feel those big moments and they  and then you give people a rest and then  you really feel another big moment and  so we were always trying to do that in  this movie and try and find those places  where we could peek and where the  valleys were and that sort of thing and  I ended up being I think pretty  effective people seem to be enjoying the  ride so so you just got out of a  screening of the film yeah what was it  like to see  this movie that you've been working on  for so long finally on an IMAX screen  yeah well IMAX is my preferred format I  was tell people to see it this way and  in particular because with this film  we've actually delivered IMAX a bunch of  additional material essentially a  different aspect ratio or a different  format for IMAX so IMAX is like  especially its it's a premium way to see  this movie and I've seen the movie a few  times in traditional theaters versus the  IMAX theater and it's more immersive  it's more impactful and you really feel  like you're in the movie so it's it's  obviously the screen is very large and  the presentation is excellent  those are important things and and  they're great but the other thing is  this additional material so we have a  movie that was formatted like this and  then when we for about half for about an  hour of the movie for 65 minutes we pop  open to a bigger image so physically get  more pixels so it's actually additional  visual effects material it's additional  characters faces and stuff alternate  shots it's all it's a special IMAX  presentation we put a lot of time and  effort into that experience and it  really pays off I think I mean I feel  like it's it's the only way to see it I  wish there were more IMAX screens  because then more people could see it  but it's really an amazing it's amazing  presentation so we're really proud of it  I know that the post guys here you know  they take so much pride and wanting to  deliver to you guys the what you  intended as filmmakers you know for  audience members what does that mean to  you like you know that relationship  between the filmmakers and IMAX and you  know what we strive to to deliver to you  guys well it's interesting when we  deliver these movies because what we've  done we've had this relationship with  IMAX over many years and and what we've  been able to do is develop is with any  any other presentation that you're gonna  see so if you go out to any theater  you're gonna see conceivably a very nice  presentation and a very nice theater  with leather seats and all that stuff  and that's great but what they've done  at IMAX  they taken our movie and actually  reprocessed it to actually reduce the  noise  increase the sharpness actually enhance  the presentation on a shot by shot  artist-driven process so we monitor that  process and we work with them on it so  it's not something that's done in a  vacuum we're a part of it and it's  actually an extension of our process we  also do additional color grading here so  it's like taking the movie and just  turning it up to 11 is really what we're  doing can you talk about some of the  creative process when you guys started  first interpreting you know the original  comic book series of Steve Ditko art you  know and how did that influence your  process in creating the stereoscopic  effect yeah well look it's a it's a  fascinating set of comic books right and  and we were really excited to sort of  remove the shackles and sort of say  there's no rules now you know there's no  anything we can do and so we found this  out really when we first started on the  very first trailer that we did we had  this shot of four quadrants of a city  kind of folding in on themselves and it  was a big epic shot and and when we  received the shot it just you know to  talk about the way you would typically  do a shot like that for 3d when you're  talking about big wide vistas and big  landscapes and things like that what you  typically do is you don't put a lot of  depth into those shots because because  of the nature of the human perception  you actually would not get a lot of  stereo effect out of looking at a  horizon you wouldn't it's actually be  almost flat right and so that the the  sort of the traditional way to do a shot  like that would be a very shallow shot  actually and we said let's just they're  breaking the rules all over the place  let's see what happens if we just turn  it the other way we just turn it on its  head and say no what if we went crazy  with it make it much deeper than it  should be should be right because  they're breaking rules anyway right so  who cares and so we we went the other  way and just turned it up and just  stretched it out like crazy  and and as soon as we did that we were  like there you go that's the movie  that's the way we have to do this and  and so every time we would get a big  shot  that we would just say like how do we  stretch it how do we go take it further  and really make it a visceral experience  for the audience how do you turn it into  something that they're not expecting  that their eyeballs to do their brains  aren't expecting to process it and so  not delivering a kind of a traditional  approach but you know and this is why I  say sort of always trying to find ways  to use these tools in some new way is  like let's not take the the way that if  you had a 3d camera out on location and  you actually have four cities folding  what would it look like this is what you  would get instead we have because we're  doing is a post process we can do  whatever we want we're not bound by  physics in any way we can do whatever  let's go crazy with it and let make it  freaky and weird and different and  that's what we did and I ended up  working out really well I mean people  get chills when the shot comes on so  it's exciting that kind of answered my  next question but aside from that did  you have any specific personal goals or  goals for your team that you wanted to  achieve on this film it was about taking  people on a ride it's a rollercoaster  ride kind of movie I mean there's  definitely these slow builds and then  these big exciting I mean the magical  mystery tour as we call it which is  that's this big epic sort of a trip  through the multiverse that strange  makes when he's first learning about the  magical world that he actually lives in  it's he's immersed in it and he's  completely taken away by it and we were  we wanted to we had explored doing this  kind of thing a little bit in ant-man  with with their Microverse and it was a  shorter sequence and and not as crazy  but we had kind of designed something  like that so it was like how do we take  that but take it to another level again  just turn it up even more and really go  crazy and that's what we try to do and  so while still we don't want people you  know getting nauseous or anything so  it's how do you modulate that where's  that line how do you find it and and I'm  really happy with that sequence it's one  of the sequences people talk about  constantly it's just such a ride and  then of course in IMAX it's an extra top  and bottom material which makes it even  more crazy so it's really fun  why do you think the character and story  of Doctor Strange lends itself to IMAX  and to storytelling through that  specially formatted aspect ratio I would  say for me it's about the canvas right  this is a big canvas and IMAX is  delivering the biggest canvas that there  is out there so it's an opportunity for  us to to tell this big epic story with  magic and crazy stuff going on and do it  in a way that really does it justice I  mean yes you'll see it on a small screen  someday and your television or on your  iPhone or whatever it is someday you're  gonna see it that way but this the way  we intended to see it let's let's put it  up big bigger than you can really take  in from a 3d standpoint that what does  that extra top and bottom allow you to  do well it's interesting because what  what happens is that with 3d you're  essentially looking through a window and  that's the way that you have to think  about it it's hard to if you don't do it  every day it's hard to kind of get your  head around it but basically you're  looking through a window and what's  happening outside is the 3d but with a  movie like this anything that breaks the  window top bottom left right is gonna  mess with the illusion of depth right so  if you make the window bigger you  suddenly have much more to play with in  terms of getting things out into the  audience space or allowing the audience  to be deeper in there without this sense  of looking through a window there's  something much more visceral about being  there and being sort of surrounding you  in all directions so to me I love that  sixty five minutes of the movie because  it really feels like when it's like the  shackles are released in a way and you  suddenly are just looking out into this  infinite space it's very exciting just  to digress for a moment did you get a  chance to see the movie ghostbusters  yeah yeah and did you have a chance to  see it in IMAX yeah with the breaking  windows yeah yeah that's a totally  different kind of usage right it is I  mean we did that same effect a little  bit in guardians of galaxy we did that  we experimented with that as well and  it's a lot of fun the breaking of the  windows we're essentially or have a  matter  movie that's two four oh but you would  allow certain objects to go out beyond  beyond the mats and it's great and it's  a really fun effect and it's unexpected  and the audience gets a kick out of it  the problem with that we've found with  our movies with our content is that if  it's not a movie that the tone has to  fit right so guardians was a movie where  when you did those moments that fit with  the tone of the scene but in a movie  like Doctor Strange it would really take  you out of the movie you would become  aware of it as a gimmick so it's it's  very important so you look at a movie  like Ghostbusters perfect tone and it's  so much fun when it happens right when  the you know something with a you know  whatever a ghost vomits out at you it's  like really fun but but you're  definitely aware of it your as an  audience member you're like oh I'm  watching a movie this is happening we  kind of our goal was really to transport  people into there into this world so we  didn't want to constantly be reminding  them that oh they're seeing a trick you  know in an int benedict cumberbatch of a  couple days ago he was he was so  interesting how he described IMAX and  like the 3d and the visual effects and  he called it like a whole nother  character yeah you know and to help sort  of move the story along how do you think  those visual effects and 3d and that  IMAX you know extra expanded aspect  ratio helps the story well I think that  you know I've had the luxury of working  on the movie long enough to be able to  see it before the visual effects were  done see it after the visual effects are  done see it and the 3d was done and then  see it when IMAX has done and every step  along the way you see this sort of  dramatic rise and change in terms of the  effectiveness of the storytelling  overall so clearly I think most people  would see very clearly the visual  effects is a critical part of a movie  like this without all the visual effects  it's sort of hard to get a sense of  what's happening you know if you just  cut to a black card that said this  happened it wouldn't really work but  what's maybe not as obvious to people  until they experience it is that the 3d  does the same thing and  and and what's interesting is that we  there are folks in our organization that  really focus on the 2d part of the movie  and so they don't get a chance to come  and visit and see the 3d that often and  when we finally debuted it  they were just floored which I love you  know what I mean you want that effect of  just like they think they know the movie  these guys have been working with the  movie for a year they know what it is  and then they go oh my god I never saw  that thing I never saw that up here oh  my god that's so different than I  expected and it's all there in their  imagery all we're trying to do with the  3d conversion is take the 2d imagery and  honor it and make it better in whatever  way we can  right so there are any shots or scenes  or sequences that were particularly  challenging and and why well we had a  few I mean there's a few sequences in  the movie that were challenging for  different reasons I mean we definitely  dark dimension is a tricky one because  there's no sense of scale there I mean  he's Dormammu is humungous guy and and  how big is he I don't know it's hard to  even tell how far away is he you know  there's no real depth cues to tell you  what anything all the rules are off in  the dark dimension really so and it  turned out great but it's it was tricky  for awhile to figure out like just where  do we put things how big how deep do we  go what do we do there so that was a fun  sequence for us to sort of test with and  figure out what to do to make it  impactful and and yet still allow the  audience not to be we don't want to  distract you know we want to support the  storytelling of it so that was a big one  you know Hong Kong was challenging just  from a technical perspective  everything's going backwards and there's  tons of destruction all kinds of crazy  stuff going on and it's a tricky one too  from a technical perspective to finish  it to make it look good and clean and  all that the there's a scene at the  beginning of the film where where dr.  strange seeks out this guy Pangborn to  figure out how he's healed himself and  he meets him in a environment that has  basically fences everywhere and it's a  non visual effect sequence so we got  ahold of that sequence fairly early and  we were able to start working on it but  to separate  born from his fence from the fence  behind him from the basketball players  behind him and to get all those layers  in there in a way and then repaint all  the areas that are that are get broken  when we separate them was just a that  was a very challenging sequence to get  clean and do right that was just a  tricky tricky set of shots it's not even  a shot you would really think it's  invisible number right I mean it but  it's the thing if you do it wrong then  everybody's like what's up with that why  does it look like that so that was a  tricky one for us and as in the  invisible category for you personally  were there any particular like triumphs  or scenes or shots that you're  especially proud of that you can't wait  for people to see they're the one shot  that personally I was my thing is  there's a moment where strange and Mordo  are standing on this building and they  and they just and then the building  flips and they fall off the building  into a city that's expanding in this  sort of Hitchcock style shot that's the  building the whole city is stretching  the skyline and they're falling these  little guys falling into this into this  abyss below right so we that shot was it  was it's the deepest shot in the movie  it's it's very very deep and and the  guys you know sort of initially we had  them falling very far but then as we  started to look at it we were like now  let's not have them fall very far at all  we'll just have them they only travel a  few a little bit in depth but the city  traveled a lot in depth and so I feel  like as an audience member you're right  there with them falling kind of going  like this so I'm always excited when  people see that shot which not for  competing people have gotten to see it  yet but it's the movie rolls out people  start to experience it but it's wasn't  any of the trailers or anything it's  just a really fun shot so I get a kick  out of that shot because I watch  people's faces when they watch it and  it's definitely one where if you have  vertigo you're gonna feel it are there  any other like moments or shots that are  particularly that you're also  particularly excited for people to see I  think that or that they should look out  for yeah I mean I think I think that  there's another one that people always  miss which is real  but everybody it goes by so fast they  don't even realize what they're seeing  but there's an MC escher shot where we  start looking kind of an aerial view of  a corner of a of New York City and  strange and Mordo or running out there  and then the camera starts to shift and  as it shifts the whole street just folds  into this crazy like one Street folds up  and the other Street folds down and  there's all this crazy stuff happening  and it comes all the way around and put  into a close-up of him lots of stuff  going on in that shot very very  complicated shot and yet hopefully  people don't even realize they when you  start that shot you don't realize  anything sneaky is going on at all and  by the end of it you're like what just  happened so it's I almost wish it was a  longer shot because people don't get the  because they just sort of it goes by  people like I don't know what just  happened it's totally one of those  anamorphic effects where like it's all  about perspective yeah right yeah it's  such a cool shot I know exactly the one  you're talking about  I loved that shot so do you have any  advice for people who are trying to get  into this world the visual effects and  3d stereoscopic effects and all of that  what would you recommend that if they  were coming into Hollywood and needed  some advice I you know it's an  interesting industry it's become a more  mature industry than it used to be so  there are definitely schools that you  can go to now and this wasn't always the  case and so there are places or there  are avenues for people to start but I  feel like what serves people I always  tell people is like a some kind of  background in p